WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.340
A difficult-looking question can become much clearer

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.510
as soon as you draw the right picture.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.010
A line shows order.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.810
A table sets comparisons side by side.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.330
A sketch makes shapes and boundaries visible.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.850
An equation holds a relationship in place.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.560
Choose the one that helps you see what the question is

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:18.030
asking.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:19.860
Picture a fence.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.510
Your eye can follow every post or every panel between

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:24.280
two posts.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.580
A sketch gives both counts a place on the same line.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.690
The words each end tell us where the fence starts and

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:31.140
stops.

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:32.900
Mark those ends first.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.360
Then look for what repeats between them.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.410
A straight fence is thirty metres long.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.440
There is a post at each end, and the posts are five

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.150
metres apart.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:43.830
How many posts are there?

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:45.530
Draw the full thirty metres.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.280
Mark the left end as zero, and mark off spaces of five

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:49.690
metres.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.760
Thirty metres divided into five-metre spaces gives six

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.250
gaps.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.870
Follow each gap to its right-hand end.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:00.650
That accounts for six posts.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.520
The post at zero sits before the first gap, so add it

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:04.830
once.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:09.050
Six gaps, plus that starting post, makes seven posts.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:13.230
Now join the ends to make a closed fence, using the

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:14.640
same six equal panels.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.000
Watch the last post come back to the very first one.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:19.860
They meet at a single corner.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.370
Each panel now ends at a different post, and all posts

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:24.280
are included.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:26.840
That makes six gaps and six posts.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.760
Try the same kind of line with a queue.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.310
Sam is ninth from the front and sixth from the back.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:34.810
How many people are there?

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.970
Eight stand in front of Sam, and five stand behind.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.490
Eight, plus Sam, plus five, makes fourteen.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.520
Both ranks include Sam, so give Sam one place in the

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:45.930
picture.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.260
A table helps when there are several things to

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:49.610
compare.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.020
Box A contains eight pencils and costs sixteen

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:53.780
dollars.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.520
Box B contains four pencils and costs twelve dollars.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.170
Add a price per pencil column.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.940
The divisions give two dollars and three dollars.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:04.950
Box A costs more for the box and less for each pencil.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.360
A sketch reveals a boundary.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.550
Join two mats, each four metres by three metres, along

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:12.960
their three-metre sides.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.100
How much edge is exposed?

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.080
Each mat has fourteen metres around it.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.460
The pair has twenty-eight before joining.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:24.230
The shared edge removes three metres from each mat, so

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.860
the equation gives twenty-two metres outside.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.560
Choose a line for order and gaps, a table for

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.190
comparisons, and a sketch for shape and boundaries.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.260
Use an equation to show how the quantities fit

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:36.650
together.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:37.910
Your turn.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.560
Trees are planted every four metres along a

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.030
forty-metre path, including both ends.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:44.910
How many trees are there?

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:45.930
Sketch it first.

